  "evidence_quotes": [
    "That is the classic base-rate trap, and it is a good one to get wrong once.",
    "In the classification phase it picks the class y that maximizes P(y) · Π P(xi|y) over all attributes xi, using Bayes' theorem and dropping the shared denominator P(X) since it is the same for every class.",
    "For a numeric attribute you don't count frequencies — you estimate a class-conditional density instead.",
    "That's exactly the trap this dataset is built to expose.",
    "Laplace smoothing adds one pseudo-count per possible value and adjusts the denominator accordingly: P(v|class) = (count(v,class) + 1) / (count(class) + k), where k is the number of possible values for that attribute."
  ],
  "rationale_short": "The tutor effectively detects and addresses student errors, providing clear explanations and scaffolding. The responses are relevant, coherent, and well-calibrated to the student's level. The use of Bayes' theorem and Laplace smoothing is accurately explained, and the tutor helps the student understand the concepts through step-by-step guidance."
